1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,140
Welcome to the Fundamentals of Pathology.

2
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:06,140
The first section that we want to cover is Growth Adaptations,

3
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:10,140
and this is obviously the first section in Chapter 1 of your text.

4
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:13,140
A few basic principles before we begin.

5
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:16,140
The first is that an organ is in homeostasis

6
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:19,140
with the physiologic stress placed on it.

7
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:22,140
So we can take any example, take the heart for example.

8
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:26,140
The heart has the function of pumping against the systemic blood pressure,

9
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:29,140
and it pretty much is in homeostasis

10
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:32,140
when it deals with that physiologic stress.

11
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:37,140
Now, if there is an increase in stress, or a decrease in stress,

12
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:40,140
or a change in stress on an organ,

13
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:44,140
then the organ will undergo Growth Adaptations.

14
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:47,140
And these Growth Adaptations is what we want to discuss

15
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:50,140
over the next few minutes, and generally speaking,

16
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:55,140
the Growth Adaptations are very high yield for examinations.

17
00:00:55,140 --> 00:01:00,140
Now, the first of the Growth Adaptations is Hyperplasia and Hypertrophy.

18
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:04,140
And the idea here is that if there is an increase in stress on an organ,

19
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:08,140
this will then lead to an increase in organ size.

20
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:11,140
Basically, if the organ has to do more work,

21
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:15,140
the organ will become bigger in order to do that excess work.

22
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:18,140
Now, how will the organ become bigger? Well, there's two ways.

23
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:26,140
One way by which the organ could become bigger is to increase the size of the cells of that organ.

24
00:01:26,140 --> 00:01:30,140
And an increase in cell size is actually called Hypertrophy.

25
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:36,140
So, one way by which the organ becomes bigger is to increase the cell size, i.e. Hypertrophy.

26
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:41,140
Another way by which the organ can become bigger would be to increase the number of cells of the organ.

27
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:46,140
And an increase in number of cells is called Hyperplasia.

28
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:50,140
So, basically speaking, these two mechanisms, Hyperplasia and Hypertrophy,

29
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:56,140
allow for the organ to increase in size in the face of an increase in stress.

30
00:01:56,140 --> 00:02:01,140
Now, it's not enough to simply know the definition of Hypertrophy and Hyperplasia,

31
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:03,140
but you've got to go one step further,

32
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:09,140
and examiners like to ask about the mechanism that underlies these changes.

33
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:13,140
So, let's take Hypertrophy first. Recall that Hypertrophy is what?

34
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:17,140
Hypertrophy is when we take a cell and we make that cell bigger.

35
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:19,140
We increase the cell size.

36
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:22,140
Now, if we're going to increase the cell size, a couple important things.

37
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:27,140
First of all, recall that a cell has a skeleton, which is called the cytoskeleton.

38
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:32,140
And that cytoskeleton is what gives the cell its size and its shape.

39
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:38,140
If we want the cell to become bigger, we're going to have to increase the production of that cytoskeleton,

40
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:43,140
and the cytoskeleton is predominantly made of protein.

41
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:48,140
So, one of the very important mechanisms for Hypertrophy would be protein synthesis.

42
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:52,140
Now, obviously, in order to produce proteins, genes are going to need to be activated.

43
00:02:52,140 --> 00:03:01,140
So, you could put this story together by basically saying that one mechanism of Hypertrophy is gene activation and protein synthesis.

44
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:06,140
Another important thing that will have to occur in Hypertrophy is the production of organelles.

45
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:14,140
If the cell is going to go from this size to this size, then it's going to need the organelles that are necessary for cellular function.

46
00:03:14,140 --> 00:03:24,140
For example, mitochondria is important for cellular function, and so you will need to increase the number of mitochondria in a cell if we increase its size.

47
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:30,140
So, production of organelles is also an important mechanism of Hypertrophy, and both of these go hand in hand.

48
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:38,140
So, that's why I say here in your text that Hypertrophy involves gene activation, protein synthesis, and production of organelles.

49
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:45,140
Now, hyperplasia, on the other hand, recall hyperplasia being an increase in cell number.

50
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:49,140
So, an increase in cell number is going to involve the production of new cells.

51
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:52,140
And new cells will be produced from stem cells.

52
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:57,140
And that's, I think, pretty straightforward to understand.

53
00:03:57,140 --> 00:04:01,140
Generally speaking, hyperplasia, which is an increase in the number of cells,

54
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:06,140
and hypertrophy, which is an increase in the size of the cells, usually occur together.

55
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:07,140
So, let's take a simple example.

56
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:13,140
When a woman becomes pregnant, the uterus is going to increase in size.

57
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:20,140
Obviously, the uterus has to increase in size because it needs to provide space and a home for the developing fetus.

58
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:24,140
Now, that uterus is going to increase in size in two ways.

59
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:31,140
First, the smooth muscle of the uterus will undergo hyperplasia, i.e., there will be increased cells produced.

60
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:35,140
And second, the smooth muscle of the uterus will undergo hypertrophy.

61
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:37,140
So, the cells will also become bigger.

62
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:44,140
And, generally speaking, hyperplasia and hypertrophy will actually occur together.

63
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:49,140
One important exception to this general principle is permanent tissues.

64
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:52,140
Now, permanent tissues, they do not have stem cells.

65
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:57,140
And because they do not have stem cells, they cannot make new cells.

66
00:04:57,140 --> 00:05:02,140
Hence, because they can't make new cells, they won't be able to undergo hyperplasia.

67
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:05,140
And so, they'll undergo hypertrophy only.

68
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:09,140
This is a very high yield topic.

69
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:14,140
So, let's take a step back and remind ourselves that in the human body there's basically three permanent tissues.

70
00:05:14,140 --> 00:05:19,140
The first of the permanent tissues are cardiac myocytes.

71
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:23,140
The second permanent tissue is skeletal muscle.

72
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:26,140
And the third permanent tissue is nerve.

73
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:37,140
These three tissues, cardiac muscle, skeletal muscle, and nerve, cannot make new cells and therefore will undergo hypertrophy only and cannot undergo hyperplasia.

74
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:42,140
Here's a classic picture that likes to show up on exams.

75
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:47,140
This is actually a picture of both the left ventricle and the right ventricle.

76
00:05:47,140 --> 00:05:58,140
Here we can clearly see that the left ventricle is very thick and that the right ventricle is basically exhibiting a normal thickness.

77
00:05:58,140 --> 00:06:07,140
Now, I could show you this picture and then I could ask you, what's the mechanism by which this change has occurred within this organ?

78
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:24,620
And the answer would clearly be that this change, this increased thickness of the wall of the cardiac muscle, has actually occurred via hypertrophy because the heart cannot undergo hyperplasia.

79
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:33,620
Another important principle regarding hyperplasia is that pathologic hyperplasia can progress to dysplasia and cancer.

80
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:35,620
Now, what do I mean by pathologic hyperplasia?

81
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:41,620
Pathologic hyperplasia is when hyperplasia occurs due to some underlying pathologic process.

82
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:46,620
And this pathologic hyperplasia can progress to dysplasia and cancer.

83
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:52,620
Now, maybe we should take a step back and contrast pathologic hyperplasia to physiologic hyperplasia.

84
00:06:52,620 --> 00:07:02,620
An example of physiologic hyperplasia would be pregnancy, where due to some physiology of the organism, hyperplasia occurs.

85
00:07:02,620 --> 00:07:07,620
And there is no increased risk for cancer with physiologic hyperplasia.

86
00:07:07,620 --> 00:07:12,620
However, with pathologic hyperplasia, there is an increased risk for dysplasia and cancer.

87
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:15,620
Classic example, endometrial hyperplasia.

88
00:07:15,620 --> 00:07:23,620
You're well aware from your pathology lectures that the endometrium, or I should say from your physiology lectures,

89
00:07:23,620 --> 00:07:32,620
that the endometrium grows with exposure to estrogen, and it sheds, and it develops and sheds with exposure to progesterone.

90
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:43,620
Now, if the endometrium is overexposed to estrogen for long periods of time, the endometrium will actually undergo endometrial hyperplasia.

91
00:07:43,620 --> 00:07:50,620
And this endometrial hyperplasia can eventually progress to the formation of endometrial carcinoma.

92
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:55,620
And we'll talk much more about that when we do the gynae lectures later in this series.

93
00:07:55,620 --> 00:08:04,620
Another important point to be brought up here is that there is one exception, or one very classic exception to this general rule,

94
00:08:04,620 --> 00:08:08,620
and that is benign prostatic hyperplasia, or BPH.

95
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:12,620
Now, benign prostatic hyperplasia is a pathologic hyperplasia.

96
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:17,620
However, it is not related to cancer, and there is no increased risk of cancer with BPH.

97
00:08:17,620 --> 00:08:24,620
So, this again makes for a nice question, examiner's question, because of the fact that it's an exception to a general principle.

98
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:28,620
Okay, the next growth adaptation is atrophy.

99
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:33,620
Now, the idea with atrophy is that there is a decrease in stress on the organ.

100
00:08:33,620 --> 00:08:38,620
So, if there's less stress on an organ, the organ will actually decrease in size.

101
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:43,620
And that decrease in size is called atrophy.

102
00:08:43,620 --> 00:08:45,620
Now, again, really no rocket science.

103
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:49,620
If an organ is going to decrease in size, there's really only two ways for that to occur.

104
00:08:49,620 --> 00:08:57,620
Number one would be for the size of the cells that make up the organ to decrease,

105
00:08:57,620 --> 00:09:02,620
or number two would be for the number of cells within that organ to decrease.

106
00:09:02,620 --> 00:09:06,620
If the size of the cells decreases, the organ would decrease in size.

107
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:10,620
And if the number of cells decreases, the organ would also decrease in size.

108
00:09:10,620 --> 00:09:15,620
Both of those happen to be called atrophy, because they usually both occur together.

109
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:21,620
One, again, it's not important, it's not sufficient to simply know the definition of atrophy.

110
00:09:21,620 --> 00:09:27,620
The examiners like to go after the mechanism of atrophy, and so let's touch on that briefly.

111
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:30,620
Well, remember that atrophy is going to occur in two ways.

112
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:33,620
The first would be to decrease the number of cells.

113
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:37,620
And a decrease in number of cells is going to occur via apoptosis.

114
00:09:37,620 --> 00:09:43,620
Apoptosis is a mechanism of cellular death that we'll discuss in more detail later on in this chapter,

115
00:09:43,620 --> 00:09:50,620
but it's very important to know that a decrease in cell number will actually occur via apoptosis.

116
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:55,620
A decrease in cell size is going to occur by two primary mechanisms.

117
00:09:55,620 --> 00:10:01,620
The first is something called the ubiquitin proteasome degradation pathway.

118
00:10:01,620 --> 00:10:03,620
Now what's the idea here?

119
00:10:03,620 --> 00:10:10,620
Well, recall that if you've got a cell that's this big, and you want the cell to shrink down to something that's smaller,

120
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:18,620
in order for the cell to shrink, this skeleton, called the cytoskeleton, that was maintaining the size and shape of the cell,

121
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:21,620
has to be broken down so that the cell can become smaller.

122
00:10:21,620 --> 00:10:28,620
The mechanism by which the cytoskeleton is broken down is called the ubiquitin proteasome degradation pathway.

123
00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:32,620
Ubiquitin is a protein, I kind of think of it as a post-it note.

124
00:10:32,620 --> 00:10:39,620
It's a protein that gets posted onto the intermediate filaments of the cytoskeleton,

125
00:10:39,620 --> 00:10:48,620
and the proteasome is a organelle present within the cell that recognizes tagged, ubiquitin tagged proteins,

126
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:51,620
and then trashes those proteins and destroys them.

127
00:10:51,620 --> 00:10:56,620
And so the ubiquitin proteasome degradation pathway is a mechanism for destroying the cytoskeleton,

128
00:10:56,620 --> 00:11:00,620
and it's a high yield point to be aware of.

129
00:11:00,620 --> 00:11:07,620
Now at the same time, in order for the cell to shrink in size from this size to, for example, this size,

130
00:11:07,620 --> 00:11:12,620
you're going to end up requiring less organelles within the cell.

131
00:11:12,620 --> 00:11:15,620
And so some of the cellular components are going to have to be consumed,

132
00:11:15,620 --> 00:11:19,620
and the mechanism by which cellular components are consumed is called autophagy.

133
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:29,620
And basically what happens here is that the cell consumes its own components in vacuoles.

134
00:11:29,620 --> 00:11:33,620
So for example, if this is the cell here, little vacuoles are created,

135
00:11:33,620 --> 00:11:41,620
and those vacuoles they fuse with lysosomes, and the lysosomes contain the hydrolytic enzymes

136
00:11:41,620 --> 00:11:48,620
that are necessary to break down and destroy products into their simple building blocks.

137
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:53,620
And so basically what will happen is that an autophagic vacuole will be formed,

138
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:57,620
and the cellular components will be consumed and then destroyed by the lysosomes.

139
00:11:57,620 --> 00:12:03,620
So two mechanisms by which the cell size decreases.

140
00:12:03,620 --> 00:12:06,620
Number one, ubiquitin proteasome degradation pathway,

141
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:11,620
and number two, autophagy of cellular components.

142
00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:16,620
The third growth adaptation, which is also relatively high yield, is metoplasia.

143
00:12:16,620 --> 00:12:22,620
Now the idea here is that if the stress on an organ changes,

144
00:12:22,620 --> 00:12:28,620
then the organ will respond by changing its cell type.

145
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:31,620
And that change in cell type is called metoplasia.

146
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:35,620
Metoplasia most commonly involves surface epithelium.

147
00:12:35,620 --> 00:12:37,620
So let me step back and explain that for a minute.

148
00:12:37,620 --> 00:12:39,620
Epi means on top, and thelium means layer.

149
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:46,620
And so epithelium are the cells that line body surfaces.

150
00:12:46,620 --> 00:12:49,620
So the entire skin is lined by epithelium.

151
00:12:49,620 --> 00:12:51,620
The gut is lined by epithelium.

152
00:12:51,620 --> 00:12:54,620
The urogenital tract is lined by epithelium.

153
00:12:54,620 --> 00:12:59,620
For the most part in the human body, generally speaking, keeping things very simple,

154
00:12:59,620 --> 00:13:01,620
there's three types of epithelium.

155
00:13:01,620 --> 00:13:06,620
Recall that there's squamous epithelium, which can be keratinizing or non-keratinizing,

156
00:13:06,620 --> 00:13:09,620
but generally speaking there's squamous epithelium.

157
00:13:09,620 --> 00:13:11,620
That's the first type of epithelium.

158
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:15,620
The second type of epithelium is columnar epithelium.

159
00:13:15,620 --> 00:13:19,620
A lot of the gut, for example, is lined by columnar epithelium.

160
00:13:19,620 --> 00:13:25,620
And the third type of epithelium is called transitional epithelium or urothelium.

161
00:13:25,620 --> 00:13:32,620
And that's the epithelium that predominantly lines the urinary tract.

162
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:37,620
Metaplasia, generally speaking, involves surface epithelium.

163
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:44,620
And the idea behind metaplasia is that the metaplastic cells are better able to handle the new stress.

164
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:52,620
So that when there's a change in stress, the metaplasia occurs because the new cells that are going to be present are better able to handle that stress.

165
00:13:52,620 --> 00:14:01,620
And let's maybe highlight some of this with a simple example, which is Barrett's esophagus.

166
00:14:01,620 --> 00:14:03,620
Now, what is Barrett's esophagus?

167
00:14:03,620 --> 00:14:05,620
So let's go back and remind ourselves.

168
00:14:05,620 --> 00:14:10,620
Now, the esophagus is lined by squamous epithelium.

169
00:14:10,620 --> 00:14:16,620
And recall that there is a very sharp demarcation between the esophagus and the underlying stomach.

170
00:14:16,620 --> 00:14:21,620
And the stomach, instead of being lined by squamous epithelium, is lined by columnar epithelium.

171
00:14:21,620 --> 00:14:24,620
So again, esophagus is lined by squamous epithelium.

172
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:29,620
And the stomach is lined by columnar epithelium.

173
00:14:29,620 --> 00:14:35,620
If acid refluxes from the stomach up into the lower esophagus,

174
00:14:35,620 --> 00:14:44,620
then the reflux of acid into the lower esophagus results in a change in stress in that lower portion of the esophagus.

175
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:50,620
And that change in stress, i.e., the presence of acid within the lower portion of the esophagus,

176
00:14:50,620 --> 00:14:55,620
will result in the lower portion of the esophagus going from squamous epithelium

177
00:14:55,620 --> 00:15:00,620
to a columnar non-ciliated mucinous type of epithelium.

178
00:15:00,620 --> 00:15:02,620
Again, it's important to know these details.

179
00:15:02,620 --> 00:15:07,620
Columnar non-ciliated mucinous type of epithelium.

180
00:15:07,620 --> 00:15:14,620
And the idea here is that columnar epithelium is much better suited to handle acid.

181
00:15:14,620 --> 00:15:18,620
And so when acid shoots up from the stomach into the lower esophagus,

182
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:26,620
the cells in the lower esophagus basically change from squamous to columnar

183
00:15:26,620 --> 00:15:30,620
in order to better deal with the stress of acid.

184
00:15:30,620 --> 00:15:35,620
And this change to columnar epithelium, this metoplasia, this is called Barrett's esophagus,

185
00:15:35,620 --> 00:15:39,620
and it's a classic example of metoplasia.

186
00:15:39,620 --> 00:15:44,620
Here's a histologic image that can classically show up on exams,

187
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:47,620
and so I put this here as figure 1.2 in your text.

188
00:15:47,620 --> 00:15:50,620
This is squamous epithelium here.

189
00:15:50,620 --> 00:15:53,620
This is the normal squamous epithelium of the esophagus.

190
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:59,620
And this squamous epithelium should actually come all the way across the esophagus.

191
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:01,620
However, in this portion of the esophagus,

192
00:16:01,620 --> 00:16:07,620
we can see that the squamous epithelium has converted to columnar epithelium,

193
00:16:07,620 --> 00:16:11,620
and focally that columnar epithelium exhibits goblet cells,

194
00:16:11,620 --> 00:16:17,620
or mucin, or droplets of mucus within the cytoplasm of the cell.

195
00:16:17,620 --> 00:16:22,620
Now the next point concerning metaplasia is how metaplasia occurs.

196
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:27,620
Remember that I told you it's not sufficient to simply know the definition of metaplasia,

197
00:16:27,620 --> 00:16:30,620
but you also need to know the mechanism by which it occurs.

198
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:35,620
And the mechanism by which metaplasia occurs is via reprogramming of stem cells.

199
00:16:35,620 --> 00:16:41,620
So there are stem cells present, for example, in the esophagus,

200
00:16:41,620 --> 00:16:44,620
or in this case the lower one-third of the esophagus,

201
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:48,620
and those stem cells have the ability to produce new squamous cells.

202
00:16:48,620 --> 00:16:51,620
If they see acid, they begin to say,

203
00:16:51,620 --> 00:16:54,620
well, squamous cells aren't good at dealing with acid,

204
00:16:54,620 --> 00:16:57,620
and instead they convert themselves to columnar cells.

205
00:16:57,620 --> 00:16:59,620
And when they convert themselves to columnar cells,

206
00:16:59,620 --> 00:17:03,620
that results in the metaplasia that we've been discussing.

207
00:17:03,620 --> 00:17:07,620
Now, very important to know, metaplasia is reversible.

208
00:17:07,620 --> 00:17:09,620
That's a high-yield principle.

209
00:17:09,620 --> 00:17:15,620
If you remove the driving stress, or let's say you remove the key stress

210
00:17:15,620 --> 00:17:19,620
that was causing or that led to the metaplasia,

211
00:17:19,620 --> 00:17:22,620
that will then result in reversal of the metaplasia.

212
00:17:22,620 --> 00:17:24,620
Let's just take a very simple example.

213
00:17:24,620 --> 00:17:26,620
If we've got gastroesophageal reflux disease,

214
00:17:26,620 --> 00:17:30,620
and we've got acid shooting up into the lower one-third of the esophagus,

215
00:17:30,620 --> 00:17:37,620
that's going to result in a columnar non-ciliated mucinous metoplasia.

216
00:17:37,620 --> 00:17:46,620
If the acid reflux is ceased, for example, via some sort of therapeutics,

217
00:17:46,620 --> 00:17:51,620
then decrease in acid will result in reprogramming of the stem cells

218
00:17:51,620 --> 00:17:54,620
back to their original squamous type,

219
00:17:54,620 --> 00:17:57,620
and the metaplasia will then reverse.

220
00:17:57,620 --> 00:18:00,620
So it's very important, I think, the key take-home point from this slide

221
00:18:00,620 --> 00:18:03,620
is that metaplasia is reversible.

222
00:18:03,620 --> 00:18:08,620
Now, another important high-yield point is that metaplasia can progress to cancer.

223
00:18:08,620 --> 00:18:10,620
This is a very important point.

224
00:18:10,620 --> 00:18:14,620
And I think we all would appreciate the fact that Barrett's esophagus

225
00:18:14,620 --> 00:18:20,620
is a risk factor for the development of adenocarcinoma of the esophagus.

226
00:18:20,620 --> 00:18:23,620
And we'll talk about adenocarcinoma of the esophagus in much more detail

227
00:18:23,620 --> 00:18:26,620
when we cover the GI lecture.

228
00:18:26,620 --> 00:18:30,620
Now, one very important exception which becomes commonly tested

229
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:35,620
is that apocrine metaplasia does not increase the risk for cancer.

230
00:18:35,620 --> 00:18:37,620
Okay, what's apocrine metaplasia?

231
00:18:37,620 --> 00:18:40,620
Apocrine metaplasia is one of the changes that's seen in association

232
00:18:40,620 --> 00:18:43,620
with fibrocystic change of the breast.

233
00:18:43,620 --> 00:18:47,620
The fibrocystic changes can be associated with an increased risk

234
00:18:47,620 --> 00:18:50,620
for the future development of breast cancer.

235
00:18:50,620 --> 00:18:56,620
However, apocrine metaplasia does not increase the risk for future breast cancer.

236
00:18:56,620 --> 00:19:00,620
And that's important because although it's a metaplasia,

237
00:19:00,620 --> 00:19:03,620
it does not increase the risk for cancer.

238
00:19:03,620 --> 00:19:06,620
So please keep that in the back of your mind.

239
00:19:06,620 --> 00:19:12,620
Another important principle concerning metaplasia is that vitamin A deficiency can result in metaplasia.

240
00:19:12,620 --> 00:19:15,620
Before you can understand this, we need to go take a step back

241
00:19:15,620 --> 00:19:19,620
and maybe stick a couple other high-yield points in here.

242
00:19:19,620 --> 00:19:21,620
Let's start with vitamin A deficiency.

243
00:19:21,620 --> 00:19:23,620
So when you think about vitamin A deficiency,

244
00:19:23,620 --> 00:19:28,620
the very first sort of thing that comes to mind is night blindness.

245
00:19:28,620 --> 00:19:33,620
And I think that we're all familiar with the fact that if someone is vitamin A deficient,

246
00:19:33,620 --> 00:19:36,620
there's an increased risk for night blindness.

247
00:19:36,620 --> 00:19:40,620
Another important feature of vitamin A deficiency to bear in mind

248
00:19:40,620 --> 00:19:49,620
is that patients who are vitamin A deficient, they can become deficient in their immune system as well.

249
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:54,620
And vitamin A is necessary for proper maturation of the cells of the immune system.

250
00:19:54,620 --> 00:19:59,620
A classic example, maybe you recall this,

251
00:19:59,620 --> 00:20:07,620
when patients get a 15-17 translocation, they develop a disease that's very high yield,

252
00:20:07,620 --> 00:20:10,620
which is called acute promyelocytic leukemia.

253
00:20:10,620 --> 00:20:12,620
Now let's go back and remind ourselves.

254
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:20,620
The 15-17 translocation involves the vitamin A receptor, retinoic acid receptor in fact.

255
00:20:20,620 --> 00:20:25,620
And so when that 15-17 translocation occurs, it disrupts the vitamin A receptor,

256
00:20:25,620 --> 00:20:32,620
and that disruption of vitamin A receptor causes the cells to remain trapped in the blast state,

257
00:20:32,620 --> 00:20:37,620
and therefore they accumulate, resulting in a promyelocytic leukemia.

258
00:20:37,620 --> 00:20:41,620
And if you want to dig a little deeper, you can remind yourselves that from pharmacology,

259
00:20:41,620 --> 00:20:47,620
the treatment of, one of the treatments for PML or promyelocytic leukemia is Atra.

260
00:20:47,620 --> 00:20:52,620
Atra is also known as all transretinoic acid, and it's a derivative of vitamin A.

261
00:20:52,620 --> 00:20:57,620
So this derivative of vitamin A has the ability to bind the mutated receptor,

262
00:20:57,620 --> 00:21:03,620
which then allows the cells to actually mature and become neutrophils.

263
00:21:03,620 --> 00:21:15,620
I just go off on that tangent because it allows me to remember that vitamin A is necessary for maturation of the immune system.

264
00:21:15,620 --> 00:21:16,620
That's the second principle.

265
00:21:16,620 --> 00:21:22,620
And by the way, we'll talk about promyelocytic leukemia in much more detail when we do the leukemia lectures.

266
00:21:22,620 --> 00:21:28,620
The third consequence of vitamin A deficiency is that it can result in menoplasia,

267
00:21:28,620 --> 00:21:30,620
and that's what we want to talk about here.

268
00:21:30,620 --> 00:21:40,620
Now, let me just take you back a step and remind you that vitamin A is necessary for maintenance of specialized epithelia in the body.

269
00:21:40,620 --> 00:21:43,620
For example, the conjunctiva of the eye.

270
00:21:43,620 --> 00:21:50,620
The conjunctiva of the eye refers to a very, very thin, delicate membrane that covers the surface of the eye,

271
00:21:50,620 --> 00:21:53,620
and for the most part, we don't even appreciate that membrane.

272
00:21:53,620 --> 00:21:57,620
The time that you recognize that that membrane is there is when it becomes inflamed,

273
00:21:57,620 --> 00:22:03,620
and so for example, when a patient gets a cold and they come in with pinkness or redness to the eye,

274
00:22:03,620 --> 00:22:09,620
that is an example of the conjunctiva becoming inflamed and hence becoming red.

275
00:22:09,620 --> 00:22:12,620
But for the most part, the conjunctiva is not visible.

276
00:22:12,620 --> 00:22:17,620
It's a very thin, delicate membrane that overlies the surface of the eye.

277
00:22:17,620 --> 00:22:22,620
The conjunctiva is actually a highly specialized squamous epithelium,

278
00:22:22,620 --> 00:22:29,620
and that highly specialized squamous epithelium requires vitamin A in order for it to remain in that state.

279
00:22:29,620 --> 00:22:36,620
If there's a vitamin A deficiency, that can then result in metaplasia of those cells,

280
00:22:36,620 --> 00:22:43,620
and metaplasia of those cells that line the surface of the eye can then result in a thickness,

281
00:22:43,620 --> 00:22:48,620
in a thickening of that surface, which is called keratomalacia.

282
00:22:48,620 --> 00:22:50,620
I'll show you a classic picture.

283
00:22:50,620 --> 00:22:52,620
This is a picture of the conjunctiva of the eye.

284
00:22:52,620 --> 00:22:58,620
Now normally, the conjunctiva of the eye should be absolutely transparent.

285
00:22:58,620 --> 00:23:01,620
However, we can see here that it's become thicker,

286
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:04,620
and it's become thicker because there's a vitamin A deficiency,

287
00:23:04,620 --> 00:23:12,620
and the vitamin A deficiency results in an inability to maintain that highly specialized surface that covers the eye.

288
00:23:12,620 --> 00:23:14,620
Again, this is relatively high yield.

289
00:23:14,620 --> 00:23:22,620
The next principle concerning metaplasia is that mesenchymal tissues can also undergo metaplasia.

290
00:23:22,620 --> 00:23:23,620
Alright, take a step back.

291
00:23:23,620 --> 00:23:25,620
So what are mesenchymal tissues?

292
00:23:25,620 --> 00:23:30,620
Well, the mesenchymal tissues include things like bone, blood vessel, fat, cartilage.

293
00:23:30,620 --> 00:23:34,620
These are all mesenchymal, also known as connective tissues.

294
00:23:34,620 --> 00:23:38,620
Now the mesenchymal tissue can also undergo metaplasia,

295
00:23:38,620 --> 00:23:41,620
and the classic example is myositis ossificans.

296
00:23:41,620 --> 00:23:43,620
So what happens in myositis ossificans?

297
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:54,620
Well, in myositis ossificans, inflammation of the skeletal muscle results in a metaplastic production of bone

298
00:23:54,620 --> 00:23:56,620
within the skeletal muscle.

299
00:23:56,620 --> 00:23:58,620
Now why would the skeletal muscle be inflamed?

300
00:23:58,620 --> 00:23:59,620
It's usually due to trauma.

301
00:23:59,620 --> 00:24:02,620
So a patient undergoes trauma of the skeletal muscle,

302
00:24:02,620 --> 00:24:06,620
and when the skeletal muscle is healing, there's a certain degree of inflammation,

303
00:24:06,620 --> 00:24:11,620
and as part of that healing, the skeletal muscle actually converts into bone,

304
00:24:11,620 --> 00:24:14,620
and some bone is produced within the skeletal muscle.

305
00:24:14,620 --> 00:24:19,620
That's called myositis ossificans, and that is relatively high yield as well.

306
00:24:19,620 --> 00:24:24,620
This is a classic picture, it's figure 1.4 in your text,

307
00:24:24,620 --> 00:24:29,620
and this circular area here that looks ossified within the muscle,

308
00:24:29,620 --> 00:24:37,620
this is the myositis ossificans, or the presence of bone within skeletal muscle.

309
00:24:37,620 --> 00:24:42,620
Now be careful because examiners like to tell you or make you think that,

310
00:24:42,620 --> 00:24:46,620
or confuse you perhaps, that this is actually an osteosarcoma.

311
00:24:46,620 --> 00:24:50,620
However, notice that number one, this bone is normal,

312
00:24:50,620 --> 00:24:53,620
the bone adjacent to it is normal, and number two,

313
00:24:53,620 --> 00:24:57,620
if you look very carefully, you can see that there is a distinct separation

314
00:24:57,620 --> 00:25:04,620
of this bony metaplasia with the actual bone next to it,

315
00:25:04,620 --> 00:25:08,620
highlighting that this is actually occurring within the muscle,

316
00:25:08,620 --> 00:25:10,620
and it's not growing off the bone.

317
00:25:10,620 --> 00:25:13,620
And osteosarcoma, which is a malignant bone tumor,

318
00:25:13,620 --> 00:25:16,620
would actually grow off the bone.

319
00:25:16,620 --> 00:25:19,620
So that's an important image to be familiar with.

320
00:25:19,620 --> 00:25:23,620
The next principle in growth adaptations is dysplasia,

321
00:25:23,620 --> 00:25:26,620
and dys means bad, and plasia means growth.

322
00:25:26,620 --> 00:25:31,620
And the idea with dysplasia is that there is disordered cellular growth.

323
00:25:31,620 --> 00:25:35,620
For the most part, the term dysplasia is used to refer to

324
00:25:35,620 --> 00:25:38,620
a proliferation of precancerous cells.

325
00:25:38,620 --> 00:25:42,620
The classic example of dysplasia is CIN.

326
00:25:42,620 --> 00:25:44,620
We're all familiar with this in the cervix,

327
00:25:44,620 --> 00:25:46,620
cervical intraepithelial neoplasia.

328
00:25:46,620 --> 00:25:52,620
Recall that CIN can be subdivided into three types, CIN1, CIN2, and CIN3.

329
00:25:52,620 --> 00:25:58,620
And it's important to remember that CIN is actually dysplasia.

330
00:25:58,620 --> 00:26:02,620
Dysplasia usually arises from long-standing pathologic hyperplasia.

331
00:26:02,620 --> 00:26:06,620
What would be an example of long-standing pathologic hyperplasia?

332
00:26:06,620 --> 00:26:10,620
Endometrial hyperplasia, which can eventually undergo dysplasia

333
00:26:10,620 --> 00:26:13,620
and then become endometrial carcinoma.

334
00:26:13,620 --> 00:26:16,620
Dysplasia can also arise from metaplasia.

335
00:26:16,620 --> 00:26:18,620
What would be an example of metaplasia?

336
00:26:18,620 --> 00:26:19,620
Barrett's esophagus.

337
00:26:19,620 --> 00:26:22,620
Barrett's esophagus can become dysplastic,

338
00:26:22,620 --> 00:26:27,620
and the dysplasia can eventually become adenocarcinoma of the esophagus.

339
00:26:27,620 --> 00:26:32,620
Now, important to remember that dysplasia is reversible.

340
00:26:32,620 --> 00:26:34,620
I can't stress this enough.

341
00:26:34,620 --> 00:26:36,620
Dysplasia is reversible.

342
00:26:36,620 --> 00:26:41,620
If you remove the inciting stress, the dysplasia can potentially reverse.

343
00:26:41,620 --> 00:26:44,620
For example, if a patient has gastroesophageal reflux disease

344
00:26:44,620 --> 00:26:52,620
and the GERD is treated, then the dysplasia can actually reverse.

345
00:26:52,620 --> 00:26:56,620
If the stress persists, the dysplasia can progress to carcinoma,

346
00:26:56,620 --> 00:26:59,620
and carcinoma is irreversible.

347
00:26:59,620 --> 00:27:02,620
This is the key distinction between dysplasia and cancer.

348
00:27:02,620 --> 00:27:08,620
That is, dysplasia is reversible, and cancer is not reversible.

349
00:27:08,620 --> 00:27:12,620
A couple more quote-unquote plasias that we should discuss.

350
00:27:12,620 --> 00:27:16,620
They're not really growth adaptations, but I threw them in here

351
00:27:16,620 --> 00:27:19,620
because they fit well with the discussions that we've been having.

352
00:27:19,620 --> 00:27:22,620
The first is aplasia.

353
00:27:22,620 --> 00:27:25,620
A means without, and plasia means growth.

354
00:27:25,620 --> 00:27:33,620
And this is a failure of cell production or organ growth during embryogenesis.

355
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:36,620
And the classic example is unilateral renal agenesis,

356
00:27:36,620 --> 00:27:41,620
where a patient fails to develop one kidney during embryogenesis.

357
00:27:41,620 --> 00:27:46,620
Another important entity to be familiar with is hypoplasia,

358
00:27:46,620 --> 00:27:50,620
or another important term, I should say, to be familiar with is hypoplasia.

359
00:27:50,620 --> 00:27:53,620
Hypo means low, and plasia means growth.

360
00:27:53,620 --> 00:27:57,620
And so in hypoplasia, there is a decrease in cell production during embryogenesis.

361
00:27:57,620 --> 00:28:00,620
This results in a relatively small organ.

362
00:28:00,620 --> 00:28:04,620
The classic example would be a streak ovary in Turner Syndrome,

363
00:28:04,620 --> 00:28:07,620
and that's also an example of hypoplasia.

364
00:28:07,620 --> 00:28:10,620
All right, so that concludes this section.

365
00:28:10,620 --> 00:28:14,620
A lot of high yield, a lot of important things to be familiar with.

366
00:28:14,620 --> 00:28:18,620
Again, important to know terms, important to know mechanisms behind those terms,

367
00:28:18,620 --> 00:28:20,620
and important to know examples.

368
00:28:20,620 --> 00:28:24,620
Luckily, we're going to have many opportunities to review some of these principles

369
00:28:24,620 --> 00:28:26,620
as we go through the remainder of the text.

370
00:28:26,620 --> 00:28:30,620
So why don't you take some time to review what we've covered,

371
00:28:30,620 --> 00:28:33,620
and I'll see you when we get back to the next section.

